        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: A Dehumidifier Works on Two Fronts
          </h2>

          <p>
            The house centipede, Scutigera coleoptrata, is the fast, many-legged creature that sprints across basement floors and bathroom walls. Penn State Extension describes it as a body one to one and a half inches long with 15 pairs of long legs, giving an overall span of three to four inches with legs and antennae. It is not an insect, and that matters, because the things that drive it into your house are different from the things that drive in silverfish, ants or beetles.
          </p>

          <p>
            A house centipede needs two things from a building: damp, dark places to shelter during the day and a steady supply of small arthropods to eat at night. Take away either and the house becomes a poor place to live. A dehumidifier takes away some of both. Dry air makes the centipede itself lose water faster, pushing it out of exposed areas, and it makes the basement hostile to the moisture-loving insects that centipedes hunt, so over a season the food supply shrinks too.
          </p>

          <p>
            That second effect is why dehumidifying works better on centipedes than it first appears. You are not just changing the air for one species; you are pulling the base out from under a small food chain. Penn State Extension lists using dehumidifiers among its recommendations for house centipedes, alongside grading soil so water drains away from the foundation, and the University of Minnesota Extension notes the species is often associated with moisture, around sinks, basins and tubs.
          </p>

          <p>
            The limit is that centipedes shelter where a portable dehumidifier cannot reach: inside the hollow cores of concrete block walls, beneath slabs, in floor drains without water traps and in damp crawl spaces. Those pockets stay damp for a long time after the open room dries. That is why sealing is not an optional extra in this plan; it is the step that closes off the refuges the dehumidifier cannot dry.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">It works, but slowly, and only if you also cut the food.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A dehumidifier makes the basement drier for centipedes and for the insects they eat. Sealing drains, sumps and block walls closes the hiding places it cannot reach.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="moisture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why House Centipedes Need Damp Places
          </h2>

          <p>
            Centipedes are less waterproof than insects. Most insects carry a waxy layer on their exoskeleton that slows water loss, and many can close the breathing openings along their bodies. Centipedes are far less well protected in this respect, so they dry out quickly in dry air. That one fact explains nearly everything about where you find them: under things, in cracks, near drains and in the dampest rooms of the house, coming out only at night when they can hunt without spending long in exposed air.
          </p>

          <p>
            Penn State Extension lists their typical daytime hiding spots in buildings: beneath concrete slabs, inside cement block walls, in drains without water traps and in damp, cool crawl spaces. Upstairs, they turn up in bathrooms and around tubs and sinks, which is the moisture association the University of Minnesota Extension describes. The ones you see in the bathtub have usually come looking for damp and cannot climb the smooth sides back out.
          </p>

          <p>
            They also breed indoors. Penn State notes that in Pennsylvania house centipedes do not survive winters outdoors but readily reproduce in heated structures, and that females can live several years and produce up to 150 young. A centipede population in a damp basement is therefore not just wandering in from the garden each night. It can be a resident colony that has been living in the walls for years, which is why one summer of drying the air rarely ends the problem by itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Seasonality adds a twist. Minnesota Extension notes that house centipedes are present all year but most common in spring and summer. Spring is also when basements are often at their dampest from snowmelt and rain, and summer is when outdoor humidity pushes indoor humidity up. If you see more centipedes from April to September, that is consistent with both their natural cycle and your basement humidity, and it is the season to have a dehumidifier running.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are not sure how damp your basement is, measure it before blaming anything. A cheap digital hygrometer left on a shelf for a week will tell you whether the air sits at 50 percent or at 75. Persistent readings above 60 percent mean the space is comfortable for centipedes and their prey, and our guide to <a href="/basement-moisture-control/">basement moisture control</a> covers how to find where the water is coming from.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Centipedes Are a Symptom: Follow the Prey
          </h2>

          <p>
            Penn State Extension gives the house centipede diet as silverfish, firebrats, carpet beetle larvae, cockroaches, spiders and other small arthropods, and it makes a point worth repeating: frequent sightings may signify a greater problem than the presence of the centipedes themselves. A predator stays where food is plentiful. If you see centipedes most nights, there is a population of something else living in your basement to support them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finding out what is the most useful step you can take, and it costs a pack of sticky traps. Penn State recommends placing them around the home as monitors to learn which arthropods are present. Put them flat along walls, in corners, under the basement stairs, beside stored boxes and near the floor drain and sump. After a week or two, look at what is stuck. Silverfish, spiders, crickets, beetle larvae and the odd centipede will tell you which food source to attack.
          </p>

          <p>
            Silverfish are one of the most common results in damp basements, and they are in many ways the ideal centipede food: abundant, slow-breeding prey that lives in exactly the same damp, cluttered places. If your traps fill with them, treat that problem directly; our guide to <a href="/does-a-dehumidifier-get-rid-of-silverfish/">whether a dehumidifier gets rid of silverfish</a> covers the food removal, sealing and dust treatments that work on them. Fewer silverfish means fewer centipedes within a season.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most of the prey list shares the same needs as the centipede: humidity, clutter and undisturbed corners. That is the real reason dehumidifying works. Cockroaches, silverfish and many spiders and their own prey all do better in damp basements, and they all decline when the space is dry and tidy. Clearing cardboard boxes off the floor, storing items in sealed plastic bins and vacuuming along walls removes both the food those insects eat and the cover centipedes hunt in.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your traps catch tiny flies rather than crawling insects, that is also a moisture signal, often drain flies or fungus gnats breeding in damp drains, soil or organic debris. They are small prey, but they point at the same wet spots. Our guide to <a href="/small-black-flying-bugs-in-house/">small black flying bugs in the house</a> helps identify them and find where they are breeding.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
