        <div id="dehumidifier" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Setting Up a Dehumidifier to Dry Their Hiding Places
          </h2>

          <p>
            Target 45 to 50 percent relative humidity and hold it through the damp months. That range is low enough to make the open basement unwelcoming to centipedes and their prey, it is in line with general guidance for limiting mould and dust mites, and it does not drive running costs up the way chasing 35 percent would. The goal is consistency: a basement that swings between 45 percent and 70 percent every few days is still giving centipedes the damp nights they need.
          </p>

          <p>
            That makes drainage more important than capacity. A dehumidifier that fills its bucket at two in the morning and switches off lets humidity climb straight back up, which is exactly the hour centipedes are active. Run a hose to a floor drain or sump pit, or use a unit with a built-in pump. Our <a href="/how-to-choose-a-dehumidifier/">guide to choosing a dehumidifier</a> covers capacity, drainage and cold-basement performance, and the <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">best dehumidifiers for a basement</a> are the units we would put on this job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Place the machine where it moves air past the problem areas rather than in the middle of the most convenient floor space. If you see centipedes most along one wall, near the sump or by the laundry, put the unit nearer there with clearance on its intake and exhaust sides. Pull stored items several inches away from exterior walls so air can reach the base of the wall, and get boxes onto shelves. Every damp, undisturbed gap you remove is a hiding place gone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Crawl spaces need a different approach. Penn State specifically lists damp, cool crawl spaces among house centipede hiding places, and a standard portable dehumidifier struggles in the low temperatures and dirt-floor humidity there. A sealed vapour barrier over the soil and a low-temperature crawl space unit are the usual answer; our <a href="/best-crawl-space-dehumidifier/">crawl space dehumidifier picks</a> cover the units built for it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Upstairs, dehumidifying is usually a ventilation question. Bathrooms produce the humidity centipedes are drawn to in bursts, and a working exhaust fan run for 20 minutes after every shower handles most of it. Fix dripping taps and leaks under sinks, and insulate cold water pipes that sweat in summer; Penn State lists covering pipes that sweat as a moisture control step. A small dehumidifier is only needed upstairs when a room stays above 60 percent despite those fixes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sealing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Seal the Way In: Drains, Sumps, Block Walls and Doors
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealing is where most of the long-term benefit comes from, because it closes the damp refuges a dehumidifier cannot reach. Penn State Extension lists three specific steps: fill cracks in concrete slabs and block walls, screen and caulk sump pump covers, and install window screening over basement floor drains. None of these is expensive, and together they close the routes centipedes most often use to move between the walls, the soil and your living space.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with drains and the sump. A floor drain that has dried out has no water in its trap, which leaves an open, damp pipe straight to the soil and drain lines below. Pour a bucket of water into floor drains every few weeks to keep the trap full, and lay fine screening over the grate. A sump pit with a loose or open lid is a damp, dark cave; fit a sealed, gasketed lid and caulk around the discharge pipe and any cable penetrations.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concrete block walls deserve special attention, because their hollow cores are exactly the kind of damp, protected space centipedes live in. Seal cracks, gaps around pipe penetrations, the joint where the wall meets the floor and any open cores at the top of the wall. Our guides to <a href="/basement-crack-repair/">basement crack repair</a> and <a href="/waterproofing-cinder-block-basement-walls/">waterproofing cinder block basement walls</a> cover the materials and methods.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then work around the perimeter. Fit door sweeps and weatherstripping on basement and garage doors, seal gaps around the sill plate and rim joist where the house frame sits on the foundation, and caulk around basement window frames. Outside, keep mulch, leaf litter, firewood, stones and stacked materials a foot or two away from the foundation, since they hold damp and shelter the insects centipedes follow inside. Clean gutters and make sure soil slopes away from the wall, which is the grading step Penn State recommends.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not expect sealing to be perfect. Centipedes are flat, fast and good at finding gaps, and a few will always get in. The aim is to remove the large, damp, connected spaces that support a breeding population, so that the occasional wanderer finds a dry, empty basement with nothing to eat and no reason to stay. Combined with the dehumidifier, that is usually enough to turn nightly sightings into occasional ones.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tolerate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Do You Actually Need to Get Rid of Them?
          </h2>

          <p>
            It is worth asking honestly, because the extension view is relaxed. Penn State Extension describes house centipedes as harmless, says their long legs and speed are what frighten people, and states in its integrated pest management guidance that there is generally no reason to treat for them. The University of Maryland Extension suggests that if you see one indoors and cannot live with it, you can simply escort it outdoors.
          </p>

          <p>
            They do not damage food, fabrics, wood or the structure of the house, and they eat the insects many people would pay to remove: silverfish, cockroaches, spiders and carpet beetle larvae. A few house centipedes in a basement are, in a narrow sense, free pest control. The case for removing them is mostly that people find them alarming, and that a large population signals a moisture and prey problem worth fixing anyway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bites are uncommon. The University of Minnesota Extension notes they are rare and happen when a centipede is handled carelessly. House centipedes do have venom for subduing prey, and a bite is generally described as similar to a mild bee sting. Wash a bite with soap and water. If you see signs of an allergic reaction, such as swelling that spreads, hives or difficulty breathing, seek medical care promptly; if you are unsure, call your doctor.
          </p>

          <p>
            So treat a single centipede in the bathtub as a reminder to check the bathroom fan rather than an emergency. Treat several a night in the basement as a sign of damp and plenty of prey, and work through the moisture, food and sealing steps on this page. Either way, the response is the same: make the house dry and unrewarding, and the centipedes go elsewhere without any need to wage war on them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="treatments" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Traps, Dusts and When to Call a Professional
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sticky traps will catch some centipedes, but their main value is as a monitor, both for the prey population and for whether your changes are working. Count what they catch in the first fortnight, make the moisture and sealing changes, and count again a month later. A falling count of silverfish and spiders tells you the centipedes are losing their food supply, which matters more than how many centipedes the traps themselves hold.
          </p>

          <p>
            Penn State lists boric acid and diatomaceous earth dusts, and synthetic pyrethroid sprays such as those containing cyfluthrin or permethrin, for crack and crevice treatment where centipedes are suspected. The key words are crack and crevice: a light application into the gaps along the wall base, around the sump and into openings in block walls, not a broadcast spray across the floor. Diatomaceous earth works by drying out arthropods and loses effect when damp, so it pairs well with a dehumidified space and poorly with a wet one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Always follow the product label, keep children and pets away from treated areas, and remember the honest limit: insecticides on their own are temporary in a damp basement. If the moisture and prey stay, new centipedes move in from the walls and the garden to replace the ones you killed. Treatment works best as a short boost at the start of the plan, applied after you have dried the space and sealed what you can.
          </p>

          <p>
            Call a licensed pest control professional if sightings stay frequent after two to three months of dehumidifying, sealing and prey reduction, if your traps show cockroaches, or if you cannot find where the population is living. A professional can identify specimens, as Penn State notes, inspect voids and crawl spaces you cannot reach, and apply products not sold to homeowners. For most households, though, the dehumidifier and a weekend of sealing do the job.
          </p>

