        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying Without Fixing the Moisture</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Insecticide kills the centipedes it touches, but in a damp basement full of prey new ones move in from the walls and garden within weeks. Treat spraying as a short-term boost after you have dried the space and sealed what you can, not as the plan itself. The lasting result comes from moisture and food control.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Killing Centipedes but Ignoring Their Prey</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Centipedes stay where food is plentiful. If you remove the centipedes but leave a basement full of silverfish, spiders and cockroaches, the food supply attracts the next generation. Use sticky traps to find out what they are eating and treat that problem directly, starting with damp cardboard storage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Floor Drains Dry and the Sump Open</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A floor drain with no water in its trap is an open, damp pipe to the soil, and an unsealed sump pit is a ready-made shelter. Top up drain traps with water every few weeks, screen the grates, and fit a sealed lid on the sump. Penn State lists both as specific centipede controls.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the Dehumidifier Stop on a Full Bucket</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A unit that fills its bucket overnight switches off at exactly the hours centipedes hunt, and humidity climbs back up until someone empties it. Inconsistent drying gives them the damp nights they need. Drain continuously to a floor drain or sump, or use a model with a built-in pump.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting to Never See Another One</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Centipedes are flat, fast and good at finding gaps, and they are most active in spring and summer. Even a dry, well-sealed basement will see the occasional wanderer. The realistic aim is to remove the damp refuges and prey that support a breeding population, so nightly sightings become rare ones.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a dehumidifier get rid of centipedes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Over time, yes, especially combined with sealing. House centipedes lose water quickly and need damp shelter, and the insects they eat also thrive in humid basements. Holding 45 to 50 percent humidity makes the space hostile to both. It will not quickly clear centipedes living inside block walls or under slabs, which is why sealing those refuges matters.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What humidity keeps centipedes away?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Aim for a steady 45 to 50 percent relative humidity, measured with a separate hygrometer. That keeps the open basement dry for centipedes and their prey and is in the range generally recommended for limiting mould. Consistency matters more than the exact figure: a basement that swings up to 70 percent at night still suits them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do I find centipedes in my bathtub?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Bathrooms are often the dampest rooms in the house, and the University of Minnesota Extension notes house centipedes are commonly found around sinks, basins and tubs. They come looking for moisture or prey and cannot climb the smooth sides back out. Run the exhaust fan after showers and fix any drips to make the room less attractive.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are house centipedes dangerous?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Penn State Extension describes them as harmless, and bites are rare, usually from careless handling. A bite is generally compared to a mild bee sting. Wash it with soap and water, and seek medical care promptly for signs of an allergic reaction such as spreading swelling, hives or difficulty breathing. They do not damage food, fabric or the house.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do centipedes mean I have other bugs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually. House centipedes are predators, and Penn State Extension notes frequent sightings may signal a larger pest problem. Their prey includes silverfish, firebrats, carpet beetle larvae, cockroaches and spiders. Place sticky traps along walls and near drains for a week or two to see what else is living there, then deal with that population.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do centipedes get into the basement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Through gaps and damp openings: cracks in slabs and block walls, dry floor drains, unsealed sump pits, gaps under doors, around window frames and along the sill plate. Many also live and breed indoors year-round. Sealing those routes and keeping drain traps full of water removes the easiest ways in.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I kill house centipedes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You do not have to. Extension services generally see no reason to treat them, and the University of Maryland Extension suggests simply moving one outdoors if you cannot live with it. They eat other household pests. If you see many, fix the moisture and prey problem rather than focusing on the centipedes themselves.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

